--- body ---
## Context

Figure-generation scripts currently embed the model list and ordering directly in
code or derive it ad-hoc from the registry. This produces inconsistent ordering
across figures and forces code changes to adjust which models appear.

A dedicated `figures.toml` (or `figures.yaml`) configuration file decouples the
*visual presentation layer* from the experiment infrastructure:
- Defines the canonical ordered list of model instances to show in figures.
- References models by their `name` field from the instance registry (ticket 0156).
- Display labels come from `display_name`; this file need not repeat them.
- Supports multiple named modelsets (e.g. `frontier`, `ablation`, `full`) so
  different figures can use different subsets from the same config.

Depends on 0156 because the config references `name` and uses `display_name` from
the updated registry.

## Proposed schema

```toml
# figures.toml — model presentation layer for AEDIST figures

[modelsets.frontier]
# Ordered list of model instance names for frontier-model figures.
# Order = left-to-right on bar charts, top-to-bottom on scatter labels.
models = [
  "anthropic/claude-opus-4.6",
  "openai/gpt-5.4",
  "google/gemini-3-flash-preview",
  "deepseek/deepseek-v3.2",
  "mistralai/mistral-large-2512",
  "x-ai/grok-4.20",
  "qwen/qwen3-max-thinking",
  "moonshotai/kimi-k2-thinking",
  "z-ai/glm-5-turbo",
  "tencent/hy3-preview:free",
]

[modelsets.full]
# All models in canonical paper order (size_class then provider).
models = [
  # frontier
  "anthropic/claude-opus-4.6",
  # ... etc
]

[modelsets.ablation]
# Models used in ablation study figures.
models = [
  "anthropic/claude-sonnet-4.6",
  "deepseek/deepseek-v3.2",
  "mistralai/mistral-large-2512",
  "qwen/qwen3.5-35b-a3b",
  "mistralai/mistral-small-2603",
]
```

## Actions

1. Create `experiments/figures.toml` with at least three named modelsets:
   `frontier`, `full`, `ablation`. Order within each set = canonical paper order.

2. Write a loader function `load_modelset(name: str) -> list[ModelInstance]` that:
   - Reads `figures.toml` to get the ordered name list.
   - Looks each name up in the registry (via the 0156 loader) to get the full
     `ModelInstance` with `display_name` and metadata.
   - Raises `KeyError` if a name in the config is not in the registry.

3. Update all figure-generation scripts to call `load_modelset(...)` rather than
   building their own model lists.

4. Add a validator (or extend the pre-commit hook) to check that every model name
   in `figures.toml` resolves in the current registry.

## Test

- `pytest experiments/tests/test_figures_config.py`:
  - Each modelset loads without error.
  - No name in any modelset is missing from the registry.
  - Order of returned instances matches config order.
- Smoke test: generate one figure type for each modelset; verify axis labels match
  `display_name` values.

## Exit criteria

- `experiments/figures.toml` exists with `frontier`, `full`, `ablation` modelsets.
- Figure scripts that maintain a static ordered model list use `load_modelset()`.
  Scripts that derive models from measurement data are out of scope — forcing a
  static modelset on them would be counterproductive (as of this PR: only
  `plot_regimes_scatter.py` had a static list; others derive from data).
- Validator blocks commits that reference unknown model names (CI-level: every
  modelset entry must resolve in the registry, enforced by `test_figures_config.py`).
- Tests green.
